WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Let's talk about Brexit.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.360
The UK has been sucked into non-stop political drama 

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.040
ever since Britain voted to leave the European Union

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.440
back in 2016.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.920
Order!

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.040
The Prime Minister's talking nonsense.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.160
And now, the Brits are going back to the polls

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.600
to choose a new government.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.040
One way or another, we must proceed straight to an election.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.400
Now, anything can happen. But so far, the UK is on course

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.320
to deliver Brexit in the new year.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.040
But are all those people who voted to leave the EU

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.600
going to get what they wanted?

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.640
Why did they choose Brexit anyway?

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.520
There's a whole bunch of reasons why people voted for Brexit

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.640
in the 2016 referendum.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.520
But there was only one question on the ballot:

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.760
Should the UK remain a member of the European Union

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.080
or leave the European Union?

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.840
Simple choice, right?

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.160
Well, not so much.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.920
That's because leaving the EU is actually a highly technical process.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.280
The European Union is a club of 28 countries with common laws

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.760
about areas like security, foreign policy and human rights.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.440
And it's a trade bloc of rules and regulations

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.600
that allows the free movement of products and people.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.600
But if you're a politician, talking technicalities

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.640
is not going to win you votes.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.520
So this is how the two sides argued their case:

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.040
The "Remain" campaign which was known as "Britain Stronger in Europe"

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.920
said staying in the EU made economic sense.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.040
Above all, it is about our economy.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.080
It will be weaker if we leave, and that is a huge risk to Britain. 

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.680
"Vote Leave" campaigned to "Take Back Control" of Britain's sovereignty

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.400
saying it was being undermined by a bureaucratic beast in Brussels.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.200
European law is supreme.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.480
The European Court of Justice overrules our parliament and our courts.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.160
Ultimately, both campaigns were accused of misleading voters.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.680
Especially Leave.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.400
The most infamous claim was put on the side of the Vote Leave bus

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.960
which said: we send 350 million pounds a week to the EU

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.880
let's spend it on the NHS, the National Health Service, instead.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.200
Now, this was highly misleading. The actual figure was much, much smaller.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.040
But Remain was found to have fudged numbers, too. 

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.280
Britain would be permanently poorer if we left the European Union.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.760
Poorer by 4,300 pounds per household.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.320
Yup, that wasn't quite right either.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.880
And again, both campaigns were accused of fear-mongering.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.960
The Leavers even had a label for Remain's message.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.400
I think it's all baloney, it's all "Project Fear".

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.360
Ultimately, one of those arguments - specifically "taking back control"

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.920
resonated far more emotionally with people

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.760
than the economic arguments of "Project Fear".

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.480
"He has lost the biggest fight of his career".

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.400
"A political earthquake was happening".

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.480
"It is a moment that came as a huge shock to some as the UK woke up". 

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:06.160
On the 23rd of June, 2016, 52 percent of voters in the UK

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.240
chose to leave the EU.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.560
48 percent voted to remain.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.000
And how those votes were split across the country says a lot.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.840
Most people in England and Wales chose to Leave

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.400
although capital cities London and Cardiff chose to Remain.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.120
The majority of Northern Ireland and Scotland also voted "Remain".

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.560
Old people tended to vote towards Leave, again not overwhelmingly

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.800
and younger people tended to vote Remain.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.960
People with a university degree were more likely to vote for Remain

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.200
people with fewer qualifications were more likely

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.360
to vote for Leave.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.840
And so big divisions across the country

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.720
within even families.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.360
So we know roughly who voted which way

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.960
but the why is harder to define

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.800
especially among the Leavers.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.640
There's a lot of evidence to suggest that people were clear

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.040
on what they were voting for.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.680
The difficulty is what they were voting for

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.960
varies from person to person.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.480
So everyone had different motives, but there is a common thread here

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.560
and it's something called "Euroscepticism".

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.400
Now Euroscepticism is defined as a political doctrine

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.560
that advocates disengagement from the European Union.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.280
You'll find it in France, Italy and across the continent. 

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.480
Here's how it played out in the UK.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.120
After the Second World War, the UK stood back and watched 

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.000
as Europe grew closer during the 1950s. 

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.040
By the 1960s, the UK wanted to join what was then called

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.120
the European Economic Community.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.040
But France rejected them. Twice.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.760
Finally, in 1973, the UK became a member. 

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.680
But just two years later, the British government held

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.520
the very first referendum on whether to remain part of the EEC.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.120
And a majority of voters said "yes".

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.000
But ever since then, there's been really quite an uneasy relationship

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.040
between Britain and the EU. 

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.040
But here's the thing.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.000
Many people voting Leave weren't just sceptical

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.360
about the European Union.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.840
It was about a wider dissatisfaction, distrust and disapproval. 

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.440
They looked at the people in Brussels

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.880
and they looked at the people in Westminster

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.240
and they didn't like what they saw. 

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.000
They saw them as out of touch. 

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.160
Out of touch with what many British families 

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.040
were feeling for at least a couple of generations. 

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.200
In the 1980s, manufacturing in the UK collapsed

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.760
and millions of good paying union jobs were lost. 

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.880
At the same time, Britain deregulated the banking and finance sector

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.200
and it soon grew into the biggest industry in the country.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.520
Over the next couple of decades, Britain's economy boomed. 

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.280
The income gap grew too

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.600
but people at the bottom were able to lean on 

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.320
the UK's strong welfare system. 

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:44.600
"Shock and panic evident on the faces of those on the trading floor". 

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:49.320
Then, in 2008, the global financial crisis hit

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.000
and the government brought in austerity measures.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.760
There were fewer jobs, and cuts to social benefits. 

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.640
And for many working class people, life got harder. 

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.080
Which brings us to another factor in the Leave vote:

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.040
immigration. 

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.040
In the early 2000s, there were various new states that joined the EU

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.280
and then obtained free movement rights. 

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.320
So, there were a lot of people from countries like Poland, for example

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.160
who came to the UK and that had quite a substantial change

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.000
obviously some people think for the better, some people think for the worse

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.360
to the UK labour market. 

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.400
Enter the European refugee crisis in the summer of 2015. 

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.120
Millions of people - mostly Syrians - arrived in Greece and Italy

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.560
to seek asylum. 

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.600
Germany led the way in letting them in, and other countries

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.840
including the UK, welcomed some refugees. 

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.360
But there was plenty of fear, too. 

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.120
Leave voters particularly felt that immigration was a key issue 

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:51.240
and that they did feel it was having a negative impact on this country. 

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.840
Not just in economic terms but in social terms, political terms, cultural terms. 

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.120
We would hear in focus groups, for instance, that some Leave voters

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.120
would say it doesn't feel like a Christian country in Britain any more. 

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.880
So all of those frustrations about immigration

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.360
plus social and economic changes, and Euroscepticism

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.280
set the stage for Brexit. 

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.440
But what triggered the actual referendum?

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.400
That was politics. 

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.720
In 2013, a general election was coming up.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.920
The UK's Prime Minister David Cameron worried about losing votes

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.480
to the increasingly popular and Eurosceptic UK Independence Party. 

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.840
So Cameron promised the British people that if his

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.480
Conservative Party won

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.680
he would hold a referendum on EU membership. 

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.440
David Cameron never thought that he would actually

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.240
have to give that referendum. 

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.160
And I don't think he thought he'd win an outright majority

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.520
at the next general election. 

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.000
But he did. 

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.880
So, he had to keep his pledge. 

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.040
Cameron then campaigned to stay in the EU

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.400
and thought he would win again. 

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.440
And it's often said that any leader will not call a referendum

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.440
unless they think they can win. 

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:03.240
And David Cameron certainly thought that he could.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:07.000
Another political gamble gone bad for David Cameron. 

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.320
And in the end, that referendum became a lightning rod

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.120
for decades of built-up discontent about all sorts of things.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:16.600
In some ways, Brexit was an accident 

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.000
waiting to happen. 

